[Fact:spectrumcomputing:Spectrum Computing]
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6321"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Forest At World's End</a>
(credits, magazine scores and covertape).

[Fact:story:The Story]
You set out to rescue Princess Mara, who has been captured by the wizard
Zam. The game was written by David M. and Jean A. Banner with an inlay by
Terry Greer, and cost &pound;5.50. Sources: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6321"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>,
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6321"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:fire:You Fire An Arrow]
Crash pointed out that shooting the elf prints "You fire an arrow:-", yet
the parser doesn't know the word FIRE. The message is at #R$DEE5, and it is
printed by #R$F2D4. Crash also missed a SCORE command and found that going
east and then west doesn't always bring you back where you started. See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6321"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:chasm:Four Words For The Chasm]
Personal Computer News noted that crossing the chasm needs a four-word
command with a preposition, and Personal Computer Games finished the game in
about three hours, despite flying lizards that kill at random. See the
<a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/zxsr.php?id=6321"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">reviews</a>.

[Fact:scores:Reviews]
Crash issue 10 gave it 6/10, Big K issue 9 2/3 and Personal Computer
Games issue 15 5/10. It was later on the Your Sinclair issue 48 Smash Tape
Special Edition. Source: <a href="https://spectrumcomputing.co.uk/entry/6321"
  rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow">Spectrum Computing</a>.

[Fact:unused:Unused Messages]
Three short response messages, at #R$DF91, #R$DF97 and #R$DF9D, are never
printed.
